{
  "title": "Fourier Transform Tables",
  "intro": "The four members of the Fourier family side by side, then the common pairs and properties of the Fourier transform, the DTFT and the DFT, in one place to look things up.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "This topic measures frequency $f$ in hertz, so the transform is $X(f) = \\int x(t) e^{-j2\\pi f t} dt$ with no $2\\pi$ in front of the inverse. Books that use the angular frequency $\\omega = 2\\pi f$ write $X(\\omega) = \\int x(t) e^{-j\\omega t} dt$ and put $\\frac{1}{2\\pi}$ in front of the inverse; to convert an entry, replace $f$ by $\\omega/2\\pi$; each $\\delta(f)$ then becomes $2\\pi\\,\\delta(\\omega)$, since $\\delta(\\omega/2\\pi) = 2\\pi\\,\\delta(\\omega)$. In rules that integrate or convolve over frequency (Parseval, multiplication), $df = d\\omega/2\\pi$ adds a factor $\\frac{1}{2\\pi}$. Here $\\operatorname{sinc}(u) = \\sin(\\pi u)/(\\pi u)$, $\\operatorname{rect}(u)$ is 1 for $|u| < \\tfrac{1}{2}$ and 0 outside, and $\\operatorname{tri}(u) = \\max(1 - |u|, 0)$."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "The Fourier family"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Transform",
